      "flagged": true,
      "answer": "Objective: To get the currently logged-in user account in custom module code in Drupal 10.\n\nTo get the currently logged-in user account in Drupal 10, you can use the `currentUser()` function, which is available in the `Drupal` namespace.\n\nHere's an example of how to use it:\n```php\n$account = \\Drupal::currentUser();\n```\nThis will give you the currently logged-in user account as an instance of the `AccountInterface` interface.\n\nAlternatively, you can use the `filter_default_format()` function, which takes an instance of `AccountInterface` as an argument and returns the currently logged-in user's default text format.\n```php\n$account = \\Drupal::moduleHandler()->invokeAll('user_login');\n```\nNote that the `currentUser()` function is available in the `Drupal` namespace, and it is not necessary to use the `filter_default_format()` function to get the currently logged-in user account.\n\nVersion notes: This answer is applicable to Drupal 10.",
